    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d121">

                <pb facs="0220" n="114" sameAs="frus:frus1933-39/pg114"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">800.51W89 U.S.S.R./77: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in the Soviet
                        Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Bullitt</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1934-07-07T15:00:00-04:00">July 7, 1934—3
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>149. Your 173, July 7th. Contrary to Litvinoff’s statement to you, Troyanovsky
                    has not made the inquiry alleged and on the other hand has indicated his hope
                    that he would be instructed to take up debt negotiations here.</p>
                <pb facs="0221" n="115" xml:id="pg115"/>
                <p>At a recent meeting of the Export-Import Bank there was much talk of the futility
                    of maintaining the bank, which involves expense, if the Soviet has no intention
                    of making a real effort to effect an agreement.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
